#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# inverse_bilateral_laplace_transform

#% text
# `inverse_bilateral_laplace_transform(F, s, t)` gives the function whose two-sided Laplace transform is `F`.

#% text
# `inverse_bilateral_laplace_transform(conditional_expression(F, a < re(s) and re(s) < b), s, t)` uses the strip `a < re(s) < b`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# A rational function is inverted by residues: the poles left of the strip give the function for `t >= 0`, those right of it (with the opposite sign) the function for `t < 0`; the value is a `piecewise`.

#% item
# Without a strip, the strip containing the imaginary axis is used; simple poles on the axis give `sign(t)/2` terms.

#% item
# `exp(a s) G(s)` gives `g(t + a)`; a constant gives `dirac_delta(t)`; `exp(c s^2 + d s)` with `c > 0` gives a Gaussian.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
inverse_bilateral_laplace_transform(conditional_expression(1/((1 + s)*(1 - s)), -1 < re(s) and re(s) < 1), s, t)
#% output
#= piecewise([[(1/2)*exp(-t), t >= 0]], (1/2)*exp(t))

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Another strip of the same function:

#% cell
inverse_bilateral_laplace_transform(conditional_expression(1/(s*(s - 4)*(s - 8)), 4 < re(s) and re(s) < 8), s, t)
#% output
#= piecewise([[1/32 - (1/16)*exp(4*t), t >= 0]], (-1/32)*exp(8*t))

#% text
# A Gaussian:

#% cell
inverse_bilateral_laplace_transform(exp(s^2/2), s, t)
#% output
#= 2^(1/2)*exp((-1/2)*t^2)/(2*pi^(1/2))

#% text
# A constant:

#% cell
inverse_bilateral_laplace_transform(1, s, t)
#% output
#= dirac_delta(t)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `bilateral_laplace_transform`, `inverse_laplace_transform`
